Halo relies on the **Apache-2.0** grant, because §3 conveys a patent licence from
the contributor and this is codec code. The MIT text is retained as well so the
option stays open; both notices must continue to ship. Nothing here restricts
Halo from keeping its own source closed — both licences expressly permit
closed-source distribution, modification and resale.

## Conditions Halo is meeting

1. Both licence texts are distributed with the product, alongside this notice
   (Apache-2.0 §4 / MIT's "include in all copies or substantial portions").
   `scripts/bundle-macos.sh` copies `licenses/` to
   `Halo.app/Contents/Resources/licenses/`, so the built app, the DMG and the
   installed bundle all carry them. Keep that step in any new packaging path.
2. No upstream copyright or attribution line has been removed or altered. The
   files are verbatim copies.
3. Halo's own proprietary `LICENSE` is asserted only over Halo-original code. The
   copied tree keeps Cutlass's licence, and the `[workspace.package] license`
   entry in the root `Cargo.toml` records `MIT OR Apache-2.0` for it.
4. Apache-2.0 §4(b) — any file under `crates/cutlass/` that Halo later edits must
   carry a prominent change note, e.g.
   `// Modified by Celestia, 2026-11: <what changed>.` Unmodified files owe nothing.
   One file has been edited so far, and states its own change in a header comment:
   `crates/cutlass/desktop/Cargo.toml`, where the 53 dependency paths were rewritten
   from `../../crates/<x>` to `../<x>` to match its new location. Its `src/main.rs`
   is verbatim.
5. Apache-2.0 §6 / general trademark law — no rights in the "Cutlass" name. Do not
   market this code under that name or imply endorsement by the author.

## Not copied, deliberately

`references/cutlass/` also holds upstream's repo tooling — `clippy.toml`,
`rustfmt.toml`, `deny.toml`, `.cargo/config.toml`, `.github/workflows`, and the
fourteen translated `README*.md`. None of it was vendored, because dropping those
into this repo root would silently reformat every Halo crate (`style_edition`,
`reorder_imports`, `newline_style`) and replace Halo's lint and audit policy with
Cutlass's. They stay readable in `references/cutlass/` if any of it is wanted
later; `clippy.toml`'s five test-relaxation lines are the only piece worth
adopting on its own.

## Transitive licences pulled in by the copied crates

The tree is nearly dependency-free. Across all 68 crates there are exactly eight
external direct dependencies, verified against the resolved workspace graph:

| Crate | Version | Used by | Licence |
|---|---|---|---|
| `serde` | 1.0.229 | model, settings, storage, store, download | MIT OR Apache-2.0 |
| `serde_json` | 1.0.154 | the above plus editor, transcription | MIT OR Apache-2.0 |
| `sha2` | 0.11.1 | download | MIT OR Apache-2.0 |
| `symphonia` | 0.6.1 | transcription | **MPL-2.0** |
| `rustfft` | 6.4.1 | transcription | MIT OR Apache-2.0 |
| `safetensors` | 0.8.0 | transcription | Apache-2.0 |
| `candle-core`, `candle-nn` | 0.11.0 (git rev `12bb5d1f6350f4ea9827d9086f213feec1f0af6a`) | transcription | MIT OR Apache-2.0 |

`symphonia` resolves to thirteen `symphonia-*` packages, all MPL-2.0. MPL-2.0 is
file-level copyleft and is **not** a blocker for a closed-source binary: the
obligation is to keep its notices and state where the MPL-licensed source can be
obtained (a pointer to the upstream repository satisfies it). It attaches to
symphonia's own files only, never to Halo code or to Cutlass code. Every other row
above is permissive.

`sha2 0.11.1` sits alongside the `sha2 0.10` pin Halo already uses, so both
versions resolve; nothing was downgraded to accommodate Cutlass.

`crates/cutlass` contains no vendored third-party source and no build scripts. Upstream
`deny.toml` bans `ffmpeg-sys*`, `bindgen`, `cc`, `cmake` and `nasm-rs`, so there is no
GPL/LGPL linking path in this tree. Regenerate the definitive shipped notices with
`cargo about generate` before release — adding the crates grew `Cargo.lock` by 140
packages, and about twelve shared dependencies moved versions as a result.


## Risks the licence does not cover — recorded, not resolved

**Codec patent pools.** A copyright licence cannot grant third-party patents.
Distributing software encode/decode for `h264`, `hevc`, `mpeg2`, `mpeg4` or `ac3`, or
an implementation of Apple's proprietary `prores`, needs pool or vendor licensing
(MPEG LA, Via Licensing, Apple) independent of MIT/Apache. On macOS, routing H.264
through VideoToolbox places that burden on Apple's own terms. `av1`, `vp8`, `vp9`,
`opus`, `vorbis`, `flac`, `png`, `alac`, `gif`, `jpeg`, `webp`, `mjpeg`, `pcm` and
`mp3` (patents expired) carry no comparable royalty obligation.

**Provenance.** The upstream history is 272 commits dated 2026-10-28 to 2026-10-29 —
about 116k lines in two days, with 12 commits authored by `Cursor Agent` and 16k-line
one-shot codec commits. The licence grant is only as good as the author's ownership of
the code he licensed, so the copied tree was fingerprinted against FFmpeg, x264,
Symphonia, rav1e and dav1d on 2026-10-29. Result and method:
[`CUTLASS-PROVENANCE.md`](CUTLASS-PROVENANCE.md) — no copied copyleft code detected,
with the stated limit that the method cannot rule out paraphrase. One item is still open:
the origin of `crates/cutlass/codecs/av1/src/quantizer_matrix.bin`, a 100 KB blob that
ships inside the Halo binary via `include_bytes!`.

Neither point changes the legality of the copy as made here. Both are shipping
gates. Not legal advice.
